course: dictionary
lesson: 752
title: Battlefield and its meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A battlefield is a place where a battle is or was fought. The poem «In Flanders Fields» is about red poppies that grew on the battlefields of the First World War, so the word points to a place where soldiers fought.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «The biggest is the United Nations... so that countries would argue at a table instead of on a battlefield.» The table is the opposite of a battlefield, so the battlefield must be a place of real fighting, not talking.
explain: Battlefield can also be used figuratively, which means in a non-literal way. A messy room or a heated argument can be called a battlefield, because people or things are fighting hard. Check by swapping in your meaning: does the sentence still make sense?
fact: A battlefield is a place where a battle is or was fought.
fact: «Battlefields» is the plural, and history lessons often talk about the battlefields of the First World War.
fact: Some battlefields, such as Hastings, the Somme and Gettysburg, are now memorials that school trips visit.
fact: Figuratively, a battlefield is any place or subject where people argue or compete fiercely.
fact: Useful phrases are «on the battlefield» and «battlefield memorial».
exercise: «Red poppies grew on the battlefields of the First World War.» What is a battlefield? | A quiet park in the country | A type of flower | *A place where a battle is or was fought | A school trip to a museum
exercise: Mum says, «The kitchen was a battlefield after the cake was made.» What does battlefield mean here? | A clean room with nothing out of place | A place where a real battle was fought | *A messy place where people argue or compete fiercely | A quiet garden for picnics
exercise: «Red poppies grew on the battlefields of the First World War.» Find and copy the word that means the places where battles were fought. | World | poppies | grew | *battlefields
test: «The United Nations was founded so that countries would argue at a table instead of on a battlefield.» What is the battlefield in this sentence? | A school hall for assemblies | *A place of real fighting | Lots of tables and chairs | A quiet library
test: Which of these is a famous battlefield from history? | The Eiffel Tower | Hyde Park | *Hastings | Big Ben
exam: Which sentence uses battlefield in the figurative sense? | Poppies grew on the battlefields in Flanders | The old battlefield is now a quiet memorial | *The debate in class became a battlefield of angry words | The soldiers marched across the battlefield at dawn
